  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "section_header",
      "page": 306,
      "number": "501-II",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Mandatory Appendix 501-II Stored Energy Calculations for Pneumatic Pressure Test"
    },
    {
      "type": "section_header",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "501-II-1 GENERAL"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "The stored energy of the equipment or piping system should be calculated and converted to equivalent kilograms (pounds) of TNT (Trinitrotoluene) using the"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = [1/(k-1)]*Pat*V*[1 - (Pa/Pat)^((k-1)/k)] (II-1)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "where"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = stored energy, J (ft-lb)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "k = ratio of specific heat for the test fluid"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "Pa = absolute atmospheric pressure, 101 kPa (14.7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "psia) Pat = absolute test pressure, Pa (psia)"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "V = total volume under test pressure, m 3 (ft3 )"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "When using air or nitrogen as the test medium ( k = 1.4), this equation becomes"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = 2.5*Pat*V*[1 - (Pa/Pat)^0.286]  (aire/N2, k=1.4, SI) (II-2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "TNT = E / 4 266 920 (kg) (II-3)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "where"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = stored energy, J"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "Pa = absolute atmospheric pressure, 101 000 Pa Pat = absolute test pressure, Pa"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "V = total volume under test pressure, m 3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "For U.S. Customary units using air or nitrogen as the test medium ( k = 1.4), this equation becomes Ä"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = 360*Pat*V*[1 - (Pa/Pat)^0.286]  (aire/N2, k=1.4, U.S. Customary) (II-4)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "and"
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "TNT = E / 1,488,617 (lb) (II-5)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "where"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "E = stored energy, ft-lb"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "Pa = absolute atmospheric pressure, 14.7 psia"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "Pat = absolute test pressure, psia"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "V = total volume under test pressure, ft3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "When calculating the stored energy for a vessel, the total volume shall be considered. When calculating the stored energy of a piping system, a maximum volume based on a length of of 8 pipe diameters may be considered for any single failure analyzed."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "page": 306,
      "text": "See also paras. 501-6.2(e) and 501-6.2(f) ."
    }
  ],
